{#
  Platform receipt — the body SHARED by the two archetypes of this class.

  A FRAGMENT, NOT AN ARCHETYPE, and the extension says so: `templates/*.html` is one renderable
  document each, and this file is included by two of them — `eu_platform_receipt` (English, EUR)
  and `ua_platform_receipt` (Ukrainian, UAH). Each including template brings its own stylesheet
  on top of `platform_receipt.css`.

  ⚠️ MOCK-UPS, on the same terms as the other four in this directory: not registered in
  `ARCHETYPES`, no builder, no run, and NO `data-field` attributes anywhere — so the renderer
  collects no bounding box and nothing here becomes a label. See templates/README.md.

  🔴 WHY THIS CLASS EXISTS: THE CORPUS HAS ONE CURRENCY AND ONE LANGUAGE. The contract's own
  reference profile records it as a finding rather than a footnote — `single_valued_dimensions`,
  `currency_UAH: 1315` and `language_uk: 1315` out of 1315 documents, with the note that the run
  therefore does not EXERCISE either dimension and that a per-currency or per-language figure
  computed on it is the corpus average under another name. Foreign-currency conversion is
  declared core to the system and no document in the corpus is in a foreign currency.

  ONE BODY FOR TWO DOCUMENTS, AND THE LANGUAGE IS DATA. Every caption arrives in `labels`; the
  markup contains no printed word of either language. That is not tidiness — it is what makes
  "the same document in two languages" a fact about the render rather than a claim about two
  files that were written to look alike.

  THREE AXES SEPARATE THE TWO, NOT TWO, AND THE THIRD IS LAW RATHER THAN NOISE:

    * language — `labels`;
    * currency — the amounts and the code beside them;
    * TAX TREATMENT. 📄 A domestic Ukrainian supplier registered for ПДВ prints its identifiers
      and a 20% tax line. A platform receipt in the English-speaking practice is 👁 commonly a
      RECEIPT AND NOT A TAX INVOICE, and says so in as many words. The conditional blocks below
      are that axis, and the README declares it rather than pretending the pair is a clean
      two-variable comparison.

  🔴 WHAT AN EU RECEIPT LACKS IS ITSELF EVIDENCED, and by the same method the non-fiscal slip
  used. 📄 Article 226 of Directive 2006/112/EC gives an exhaustive list of the particulars a VAT
  invoice must carry — among them the supplier's full name and address, the supplier's VAT
  identification number and the customer's. A document that declares itself not a VAT invoice is
  a document that may carry none of those, and this one carries none of those. The absence is
  the checklist read backwards, exactly as the fiscal form was for `ua_non_fiscal_receipt`.

  ⛔ NO CONVERSION IS PRINTED, and that is the point rather than an omission. No public source
  found shows such a receipt stating a rate or an equivalent in the buyer's home currency. So a
  claim evidenced by a EUR receipt and a UAH payment carries its conversion NOWHERE ON PAPER —
  which is the concrete form of what `policy_engine._check_currency` already says in prose: a
  converted amount would be a number in the ground truth that nothing in the dataset can prove.
#}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="{{ language }}">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<style>
{% for face in font_faces -%}
@font-face {
  font-family: "{{ face.family }}";
  src: url("{{ face.url }}") format("truetype");
  font-weight: 100 900;
  font-style: normal;
}
{% endfor -%}
{% include "platform_receipt.css" %}
{{ stylesheet | safe }}
</style>
</head>
<body>
{# data-document marks the page root — exactly one per renderable template, counted through the
   include, which is how the two archetypes sharing this body satisfy that rule. #}
<div class="page" data-document="platform_receipt">

  {# The seller. 👁 A platform receipt leads with the platform's own name and nothing beside it;
     a street address and a tax number are 📄 invoice particulars, and this document declares
     itself not to be one. The Ukrainian variant DOES print both, because its seller is a
     domestic company for which they are ordinary requisites. #}
  <header class="head">
    <div class="brand">{{ seller.name }}</div>
    {% if seller.address %}
    <div class="muted">{{ seller.address }}</div>
    {% endif %}
    {% if seller.tax_code %}
    <div class="muted">{{ seller.tax_code_label }} {{ seller.tax_code }}</div>
    {% endif %}
    {% if seller.vat_number %}
    <div class="muted">{{ seller.vat_number_label }} {{ seller.vat_number }}</div>
    {% endif %}
  </header>

  {# 🔴 THE TITLE SAYS «RECEIPT», NOT «INVOICE», and the distinction is the whole tax treatment
     of the document. The amount paid is repeated in the largest type on the page beside it,
     which is 👁 what such a receipt leads with — the fact that money moved, before any detail
     of what it was for. #}
  <section class="title-block">
    <h1 class="title">{{ labels.title }}</h1>
    <div class="paid">
      <div class="paid-amount">{{ total }}&nbsp;{{ currency }}</div>
      <div class="muted">{{ labels.paid_caption }}</div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="meta">
    <div class="meta-item">
      <div class="caption">{{ labels.receipt_number }}</div>
      <div class="mono">{{ receipt_number }}</div>
    </div>
    <div class="meta-item">
      <div class="caption">{{ labels.date_paid }}</div>
      <div class="mono">{{ date }}</div>
    </div>
    <div class="meta-item">
      <div class="caption">{{ labels.payment_method }}</div>
      <div class="mono">{{ card_masked }}</div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {# The buyer. 👁 A consumer receipt names the buyer and their country and stops there — no tax
     number, because a private individual has none to state for this purpose, and 📄 the
     customer's VAT identification number is an invoice particular this document does not claim
     to carry. #}
  <section class="party">
    <div class="caption">{{ labels.billed_to }}</div>
    <div class="party-name">{{ buyer.name }}</div>
    <div class="muted">{{ buyer.country }}</div>
  </section>

  {# The lines. 📄 The quantity and nature of the service is an invoice particular AND 👁 the one
     part of it a receipt does carry: it is what the buyer is checking when they open the file.

     ⚠️ THE COURSE TITLES ARE INVENTED, and that is deliberate and safe rather than a shortcut: a
     course is a WORK, not a mark under which a firm trades, and config/generation.yaml admits
     invented works while admitting no invented marks. The seller's name is the opposite case and
     is a public mark. #}
  <table class="lines">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th class="col-desc">{{ labels.description }}</th>
        <th class="col-qty">{{ labels.quantity }}</th>
        <th class="col-amount">{{ labels.amount }}</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for line in lines %}
      <tr>
        <td class="col-desc">
          <div>{{ line.name }}</div>
          {% if line.period %}<div class="muted small">{{ line.period }}</div>{% endif %}
        </td>
        <td class="col-qty mono">{{ line.qty }}</td>
        <td class="col-amount mono">{{ line.amount }}</td>
      </tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  {# 🔴 THE CURRENCY CODE TRAVELS WITH EVERY AMOUNT, on both variants. The corpus prints no
     currency beside a number anywhere else, because with one currency there was nothing to
     distinguish; the moment a second exists, a bare figure is ambiguous on the page in exactly
     the way it is ambiguous in a label. #}
  <section class="totals">
    <div class="row">
      <span class="caption">{{ labels.subtotal }}</span>
      <span class="mono">{{ subtotal }}&nbsp;{{ currency }}</span>
    </div>

    {# 📄 Present only where the supplier charges the tax. A Ukrainian company registered for ПДВ
       prints the rate and the amount; the receipt that declares itself not a tax invoice prints
       neither, and printing a zero row there would assert a tax treatment rather than omit one. #}
    {% if vat %}
    <div class="row">
      <span class="caption">{{ vat.label }}</span>
      <span class="mono">{{ vat.amount }}&nbsp;{{ currency }}</span>
    </div>
    {% endif %}

    <div class="row grand">
      <span>{{ labels.total }}</span>
      <span class="mono">{{ total }}&nbsp;{{ currency }}</span>
    </div>
  </section>

  {# THE WHOLE FOOT IS CONDITIONAL, not merely its rows, and the Ukrainian variant is why: it
     carries neither note, and an unconditional `<footer>` drew its top rule across the page with
     nothing under it — a line announcing an empty block. The A4 confirmation learned the same
     thing and states it in the same words; correct and ugly is still a defect on every image of
     a class. #}
  {% if not_a_tax_invoice_note or support_note %}
  <footer class="foot">
    {# 🔴 THE NEGATIVE MARKER, AND IT IS A STRING WITH A POSITION — which the Ukrainian
       non-fiscal slip's marker was not. 👁 Four independent commentators describe this wording
       as printed on real platform receipts. Where the contract's «marker text plus its position»
       field could not represent an absence, it represents THIS exactly. The two mock-ups
       together are therefore an argument about the SHAPE of that field rather than about one
       document — see templates/README.md. #}
    {% if not_a_tax_invoice_note %}
    <div class="notice">{{ not_a_tax_invoice_note }}</div>
    {% endif %}
    {% if support_note %}
    <div class="muted small">{{ support_note }}</div>
    {% endif %}
  </footer>
  {% endif %}

</div>
</body>
</html>
